+SYNTHETIC STUDENT APPLICATION — FOR VALIDATION ONLY
+Applicant ID: pilot-001
+Profile as of: 2024-11-01
+All people, schools, activities, and outcomes in this packet are fictional.
+
+Academic background
+I attend a public high school and intend to study mechanical engineering.
+My unweighted GPA is 3.86 out of 4.0.
+An older counseling note says GPA: 3.9 / 4.0, without identifying weighting.
+Intended major: Mechanical Engineering; Applied Mathematics
+
+Testing
+Submitted SAT: 1440
+I have not submitted SAT: 1490 from a later practice test.
+
+Courses:
+Term | Course | Grade | Credits
+2023 Fall | Honors Algebra II | A | 1.0
+2024 Spring | AP Physics 1 | B+ | 1.0
+2024 Fall | AP Calculus AB | A- | 1.0
+Course: English Literature; grade: 92/100; term: 2024 Fall; credits: 0.5
+
+Activities:
+Activity: Robotics Club; role: Mechanical Lead; 7.5 hours/week; 3 years; achievement: Regional design award
+I served as volunteer at Neighborhood Learning Center; 2 hours per week; 8 months.
+Activity: School Orchestra; role: Violinist
+I also look after my younger brother most weekday afternoons; my schedule varies.
+
+Personal statement:
+The robot moved twelve centimeters and stopped. I had spent three evenings
+adjusting the wheels, and I was certain the motor was the problem. A younger
+teammate asked whether we had checked the battery connection. We had not.
+
+I was captain of our first prototype team. That title made it tempting to answer
+every question before someone else could. After the battery incident, I began
+asking each teammate to propose a test before we changed a part. Our notebook
+filled with failed hypotheses, but our decisions became easier to explain.
+
+Tutoring changed my understanding of that process. When a student asked why a
+fraction became larger after division, repeating the rule did not help. Drawing
+an example did. I began bringing sketches to robotics meetings, too. I want to
+study mechanical engineering because I enjoy translating a confusing problem
+into something a team can inspect, test, and improve together.
+
+Honors:
+School service recognition, 2024. This honor is not yet represented by the parser.
